<article class="prose">
  <section id="scope" aria-labelledby="scope-title">
    <h2 id="scope-title">What this page covers</h2>
    <p>
      You run a Debezium connector against MySQL or SQL Server and want to know
      where the log position lives, how long it stays readable, what breaks
      when it is lost, and what the sink must do about the duplicates a
      restart brings. The Postgres version of this page is
      <a href="{{ '/postgres-replication-slots/' | url }}">Postgres slots &amp; WAL growth</a>;
      the setup steps are in the
      <a href="{{ '/quickstarts/quickstart-mysql/' | url }}">MySQL</a> and
      <a href="{{ '/quickstarts/quickstart-mssql/' | url }}">SQL Server</a>
      quickstarts, and
      <a href="{{ '/oracle-notes/' | url }}">Oracle</a> has its own notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thread is the same as everywhere on this site. A connector resumes
      from a stored <em>log position</em>. Offsets are committed periodically,
      so a restart re-sends some changes: delivery is at-least-once. If the
      position can no longer be read, the only correct way back is a
      re-snapshot into a sink that is idempotent: keyed by primary key and
      ordered by log position, never by a timestamp. There is no end-to-end
      exactly-once anywhere on this page.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two engines differ in <em>who holds the position</em>, and that
      decides which failure you get. Postgres keeps a server-side slot that
      pins WAL until the consumer confirms it. <strong>MySQL keeps nothing for
      a consumer that is not connected</strong>: the binlog is purged on a
      timer. <strong>SQL Server's connector does not read the log at all</strong>:
      it reads change tables that a capture job fills and a cleanup job
      empties, so there are two clocks (and two jobs) between a commit and
      your connector.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="Versions checked">
      <p>
        <strong>Checked against</strong> (read on 2026-10-09): the Debezium
        reference at <code>debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable</code>
        for the MySQL and SQL Server connectors, which was labelled 3.7; the
        MySQL 8.4 Reference Manual, plus the 8.0 manual for
        <code>expire_logs_days</code>; and Microsoft Learn pages for SQL Server
        change data capture. A few statements come from the Debezium source
        code on its <code>main</code> branch, not a release tag; they are
        marked. Where I could not confirm something, the text says
        &ldquo;check your version&rdquo;. The last section lists what was run
        and what was not.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="positions" aria-labelledby="positions-title">
    <h2 id="positions-title">The log position, engine by engine</h2>
    <p>
      Every Debezium change event carries a <code>source</code> block with the
      position of the change in the database's log. That block is what a sink
      should compare. The fields differ by engine:
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>Where the position comes from, and what the sink compares</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Engine</th>
          <th scope="col">Fields in <code>source</code></th>
          <th scope="col">What the sink compares</th>
          <th scope="col">Where it stops being comparable</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">MySQL</th>
          <td>
            <code>file</code>, <code>pos</code>, <code>row</code>,
            <code>gtid</code>, <code>server_id</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            The tuple (binlog file number, <code>pos</code>, <code>row</code>).
            Debezium describes <code>row</code> as the &ldquo;row within the
            event&rdquo;, so one binlog event that carries several rows has one
            <code>file</code>/<code>pos</code> and several <code>row</code>
            values. The <code>gtid</code> names a whole transaction.
          </td>
          <td>
            A different server (file and position are that server's own
            coordinates), or a reset of the binlog:
            <code>RESET BINARY LOGS AND GTIDS</code> restarts the file number
            at <code>.000001</code>, whereas <code>PURGE BINARY LOGS</code>
            does not reset numbering.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">SQL Server</th>
          <td>
            <code>commit_lsn</code>, <code>change_lsn</code>,
            <code>event_serial_no</code>
          </td>
          <td>
            The tuple (<code>commit_lsn</code>, <code>change_lsn</code>,
            <code>event_serial_no</code>). <code>commit_lsn</code> alone is
            shared by every change in a transaction. Snapshot events have a
            <code>commit_lsn</code> and null <code>change_lsn</code> and
            <code>event_serial_no</code>.
          </td>
          <td>
            A different database that is not a continuation of the same log
            (a restore into a new database, for example). My understanding,
            not a sentence I found in the docs: replicas in one availability
            group share one log lineage.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The tuple is the same one the
      <a href="{{ '/partitioning/#recon' | url }}">Partitioning page</a> lists
      for the version guard. Compare it as a typed tuple or as fixed-width
      text, never as free text, and never substitute <code>ts_ms</code> or a
      Kafka offset. Glossary:
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#lsn-scn' | url }}">LSN / SCN</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#log-retention' | url }}">log retention</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#at-least-once' | url }}">at-least-once</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>What a SQL Server LSN looks like</h3>
    <p>
      An LSN is 10 bytes. Microsoft documents its structure as
      <code>[VLF ID:Log Block ID:Log Record ID]</code> and gives the example
      <code>00000031:00000da0:0001</code>, where <code>0x31</code> is the
      virtual log file ID, <code>0xda0</code> the log block ID and
      <code>0x1</code> the first record in that block. Debezium writes it in
      the same shape, as hex groups of 4 bytes, 4 bytes and 2 bytes
      (<code>00000027:00000758:0003</code> in its docs), and the SQL Server
      functions return it as <code>binary(10)</code>. So the string is eight,
      eight and four hex digits, and strings of that fixed width compare
      correctly as text only if they use the same case; Debezium's own code
      prints lower case.
    </p>
    <p>
      How the three event fields map to CDC's own columns is not spelled out in
      the Debezium reference. In the connector source on <code>main</code>,
      <code>commit_lsn</code> comes from <code>__$start_lsn</code> and
      <code>change_lsn</code> from <code>__$seqval</code>; Microsoft describes
      <code>__$start_lsn</code> as the commit LSN that orders transactions and
      <code>__$seqval</code> as the column that orders changes inside one
      transaction. Debezium's reference says <code>event_serial_no</code>
      &ldquo;differentiates events that carry the same position&rdquo;: an
      update is stored as two rows (before and after) and Debezium emits one
      event with <code>event_serial_no = 2</code>; a primary-key update is a
      delete plus a create that share both LSNs and get <code>1</code> and
      <code>2</code>.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="Check your version: direct mode ordering">
      <p>
        <strong>Check your version.</strong> The connector's
        <code>data.query.mode</code> is <code>direct</code> by default (it
        reads the change tables itself, not through the
        <code>fn_cdc_get_all_changes</code> functions). Debezium's reference
        says that in direct mode a position also includes
        <code>__$command_id</code>, which is not in the <code>source</code>
        block, and the source orders by <code>__$start_lsn</code>, then
        <code>__$command_id</code>, then <code>__$seqval</code>. I did not
        verify whether the command id can ever disagree with the change LSN
        within one transaction. The tuple above is what the site's version
        guard uses; if you can reproduce a disagreement on your version, treat
        the position as unique-per-change but not strictly ordered inside a
        transaction.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <h3>What a MySQL position looks like</h3>
    <p>
      MySQL gives you two coordinate systems. <strong>File and position</strong>
      (<code>mysql-bin.000003</code>, <code>570</code>) are local to one
      server. A <strong>GTID</strong> is <code>source_id:transaction_id</code>,
      for example <code>3E11FA47-71CA-11E1-9E33-C80AA9429562:23</code>; the
      <code>transaction_id</code> is a sequence number assigned in commit order
      on the originating server, and GTIDs are unique across a replication
      topology. A GTID <em>set</em> such as
      <code>3E11FA47-71CA-11E1-9E33-C80AA9429562:1-5</code> is what
      <code>gtid_executed</code> and <code>gtid_purged</code> hold. A set is
      not a single number you can sort rows by, and numbers from two different
      <code>source_id</code>s are not comparable.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the connector, GTIDs are what survive a move to another server in the
      same cluster (see <a href="#mysql-failover">replicas and failover</a>).
      For the sink guard, the file number, position and row index are what
      order the rows of one server's log. Use the GTID to name a transaction,
      not to order it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mysql" aria-labelledby="mysql-title">
    <h2 id="mysql-title">MySQL: binlog settings, retention and purge</h2>
    <h3>Settings the connector needs</h3>
    <table>
      <caption>MySQL server settings for the Debezium connector</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Setting</th>
          <th scope="col">What it must be, and why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>binlog_format</code></th>
          <td>
            <code>ROW</code>. Debezium's table says it must be set to
            <code>ROW</code>. The MySQL 8.4 manual says the default is
            <code>ROW</code>, binary logging is on by default, and the variable
            itself is deprecated, so only row-based logging should be used for
            new setups.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>binlog_row_image</code></th>
          <td>
            <code>FULL</code> (the MySQL default). With <code>MINIMAL</code> the
            after-image of an update holds only the columns the statement set,
            so the event cannot describe the whole row. Debezium's table says
            <code>FULL</code>. The quickstart and troubleshooting pages say
            &ldquo;FULL or MINIMAL per connector support&rdquo;; for Debezium,
            use <code>FULL</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>gtid_mode</code>, <code>enforce_gtid_consistency</code></th>
          <td>
            <code>ON</code> for both. Debezium: GTIDs are not required, but they
            simplify replication and are what lets a connector move to another
            server. Required for multi-primary topologies.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>server_id</code> (connector: <code>database.server.id</code>)</th>
          <td>
            The connector joins the cluster as another replica, so its
            <code>database.server.id</code> must be unique across everything
            running in the cluster. MySQL: pick an integer from 1 to
            2<sup>32</sup>&minus;1 that no other source or replica uses.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code></th>
          <td>
            How long binlog files live. Default <code>2592000</code> (30 days).
            See below; this is the setting that decides how long a stopped
            connector is safe.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Privileges</th>
          <td>
            <code>REPLICATION SLAVE</code> to read the binlog,
            <code>REPLICATION CLIENT</code> for the status statements; the
            snapshot also needs <code>SELECT</code>, <code>RELOAD</code> and
            <code>SHOW DATABASES</code>, and <code>LOCK TABLES</code> where
            global read locks are not allowed (Amazon RDS and Aurora, per
            Debezium). On RDS, Debezium notes that automated backups must be
            enabled or the binlog is disabled.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h3>Retention: nothing holds the files for you</h3>
    <p>
      MySQL removes binlog files when they pass the expiration period.
      Removal can happen at startup and when the binary log is flushed. The
      period is <code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code>, default 30 days;
      <code>binlog_expire_logs_auto_purge</code> (MySQL 8.0.29 and later) turns
      automatic purging off, and a seconds value of <code>0</code> also stops
      it. The older <code>expire_logs_days</code> is deprecated in 8.0 and was
      removed in 8.4, where setting it is an error. Managed services have
      their own retention controls: check your provider.
    </p>
    <p>
      The crucial sentence is in the manual's <code>PURGE BINARY LOGS</code>
      page. Purging is safe while replicas are replicating, and a file a
      <em>connected</em> replica is reading is not deleted. But &ldquo;if a
      replica is not connected and you happen to purge one of the log files it
      has yet to read, the replica cannot replicate after it
      reconnects.&rdquo; A stopped Debezium connector is exactly that replica.
      The manual's advice is to set an expiration period no lower than the
      longest your replicas might lag; for a CDC connector, make it longer than
      the longest outage plus snapshot time you intend to survive, and watch
      the disk that retention costs.
    </p>
    <h3>What happens when the position was purged</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>File and position.</strong> The server refuses to serve the
        file. MySQL reports error 1236
        (<code>ER_SOURCE_FATAL_ERROR_READING_BINLOG</code>); the text after
        the colon varies and I did not verify it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>GTID auto-positioning.</strong> The server sends error 1789,
        <code>ER_SOURCE_HAS_PURGED_REQUIRED_GTIDS</code>: &ldquo;Cannot
        replicate because the source purged required binary logs. Replicate the
        missing transactions from elsewhere, or provision a new replica from
        backup. Consider increasing the source's binary log expiration
        period.&rdquo; The manual adds that the replica cannot recover
        automatically, and that reconnecting <em>without</em> auto-positioning
        &ldquo;only results in the loss of the purged transactions on the
        replica&rdquo;: the gap is skipped, silently.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Debezium.</strong> The snapshot reference says that if the logs
        have been pruned the connector fails with an error saying a new
        snapshot is required, and that
        <code>snapshot.mode=when_needed</code> makes it snapshot automatically
        when a recorded offset names a binlog position or GTID the server no
        longer has. The default is <code>initial</code>, which snapshots only
        when there is no offset (or an earlier snapshot did not finish). The
        &ldquo;Behavior when things go wrong&rdquo; section words the same
        situation differently (it says the connector performs another initial
        snapshot, and fails only if snapshots are disabled), so check which
        happens on your version before you rely on either.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="Do not skip the gap">
      <p>
        <strong>Do not &ldquo;fix&rdquo; a purged position by moving past
        it.</strong> Debezium's <code>set-binlog-position</code> signal can
        start the connector at a later position, and its docs say the
        connector then skips the change events in between and does not capture
        or stream them. That is the same silent gap MySQL warns about for
        replicas. The recovery is a re-snapshot into the idempotent sink, then
        a reconcile: a snapshot copies the rows that exist now, so a row
        deleted during the gap stays in a sink you did not rebuild. See
        <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/' | url }}">Snapshotting</a> and
        <a href="{{ '/reconciliation-surgery/' | url }}">Reconciliation &amp; Offset Surgery</a>.
        Raising retention afterwards does not bring purged files back.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <h3 id="mysql-schema">Schema history topic and DDL</h3>
    <p>
      The binlog contains DDL as well as row changes. Debezium parses the DDL
      it meets and keeps an in-memory table structure; separately it writes
      every DDL statement, with the binlog position where it appeared, to an
      internal <em>schema history topic</em>. After a restart it rebuilds the
      table structures that existed at its starting position by replaying that
      topic. The topic is for the connector's own use, and losing it is a
      separate failure from losing the binlog position: Debezium's
      <code>recovery</code> snapshot mode rebuilds it from the source tables,
      with the warning not to use it if schema changes were committed after
      the last shutdown.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>schema.history.internal.skip.unparseable.ddl</code> defaults to
        <code>false</code>, which Debezium calls the safe default. Skipping a
        DDL statement &ldquo;can lead to data loss or mangling&rdquo;.
      </li>
      <li>
        Online schema-change tools (gh-ost, pt-online-schema-change) create
        helper tables; Debezium says the connector must be configured to
        capture them, and an SMT can drop their records if consumers do not
        need them.
      </li>
      <li>
        The connector can also write schema change events to a topic for
        consumers (<code>include.schema.changes</code>). Consumers still own
        the sink-side DDL decision; see
        <a href="{{ '/schema-evolution/' | url }}">Schema Evolution</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3 id="mysql-failover">Replicas and failover</h3>
    <p>
      Debezium's topology notes: the connector follows <em>one</em> server (a
      primary, or a replica that has its binlog enabled), because it records
      its position in that server's binlog, and file and position differ on
      every server. If that server fails, it must come back before the
      connector can continue. In a highly available cluster that uses GTIDs,
      Debezium says you can restart the connector immediately: it connects to
      a different server, finds the location of the last transaction in that
      server's binlog, and reads from there. For multi-primary setups it can
      fail over only to a replica that is caught up, and
      <code>gtid.source.includes</code> / <code>gtid.source.excludes</code>
      restrict which GTID sources are used to find the position.
      <code>gtid.ignore.on.recovery</code> appears in the 3.7 reference;
      check your version before using it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two consequences for a sink. First, the new server's file and position
      are unrelated to the old server's, so a guard that compares them across
      the switch would skip or accept changes by accident. Treat each server's
      binlog as its own lineage and bump a lineage number you control at the
      switch (see <a href="#guard">the guard</a>); do not derive it from
      <code>server_id</code>, which is not ordered by time. Second, the
      connector may re-send changes after the switch, now with the new
      server's coordinates. That is the at-least-once rule again. (MySQL's own
      docs describe, for replicas, an error when the replica holds
      transactions the new source does not; I did not confirm what Debezium
      reports in that case, so make sure the new server has caught up first.)
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="mysql-runbook" aria-labelledby="mysql-runbook-title">
    <h2 id="mysql-runbook-title">MySQL triage and runbook</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Untested.</strong> There is no MySQL in this repository's test
      setup. The statements below were only parsed (node-sql-parser, MySQL
      dialect); the two <code>SHOW BINARY LOG STATUS</code> forms and
      <code>SHOW BINLOG EVENTS</code> are not accepted by that parser and were
      taken from the manual. Run them on a test server first.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- 1. Settings that decide whether CDC works, and how long a stopped connector is safe.
SELECT @@global.server_id            AS server_id,
       @@global.server_uuid          AS server_uuid,
       @@global.log_bin              AS log_bin,
       @@global.binlog_format        AS binlog_format,
       @@global.binlog_row_image     AS binlog_row_image,
       @@global.gtid_mode            AS gtid_mode,
       @@global.binlog_expire_logs_seconds AS expire_seconds;

-- 2. The oldest file still on the server. If the connector's stored file is older, it is gone.
SHOW BINARY LOGS;

-- 3. The server's current position. MySQL 8.4 removed SHOW MASTER STATUS:
SHOW BINARY LOG STATUS;       -- 8.4 and later
-- SHOW MASTER STATUS;        -- 8.0 and earlier (deprecated there)

-- 4. GTID mode: what was purged, and is the connector's set still serviceable?
SELECT @@global.gtid_executed AS gtid_executed, @@global.gtid_purged AS gtid_purged;
SELECT GTID_SUBSET(@@global.gtid_purged, 'PASTE-THE-CONNECTORS-GTID-SET-HERE') AS position_still_servable;</code></pre>
    <p>
      The last query is my derivation from the manual's description of
      auto-positioning: the source sends everything the replica is missing, and
      raises error 1789 if any of that was purged. So the purged set must be a
      subset of what the connector has already seen; <code>0</code> means
      error 1789 is coming. The connector's stored position is in its Kafka
      Connect offsets; Debezium also exposes <code>BinlogFilename</code>,
      <code>BinlogPosition</code>, <code>GtidSet</code> and
      <code>MilliSecondsBehindSource</code> as JMX metrics. Pair the lag with
      <code>binlog_expire_logs_seconds</code>: if the connector is further
      behind than the retention, it is already in the first row below.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>MySQL: symptom, check, fix</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Symptom</th>
          <th scope="col">Check</th>
          <th scope="col">Fix</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Connector fails at start; error says the binlog position or GTID is not available (1236, 1789)</th>
          <td>
            Stored file against the first row of <code>SHOW BINARY LOGS</code>;
            in GTID mode, query 4.
          </td>
          <td>
            The position is lost. Stop the connector, re-snapshot into the
            idempotent sink (<code>snapshot.mode=when_needed</code>, or clear
            the stored offset and run a snapshot), then reconcile deletes.
            Raise retention so it does not recur.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Connector is running but far behind</th>
          <td>
            <code>MilliSecondsBehindSource</code> and the connector's
            <code>BinlogFilename</code> against the newest file in
            <code>SHOW BINARY LOGS</code>, and against
            <code>expire_seconds</code>.
          </td>
          <td>
            Fix throughput (Kafka, sink, snapshot contention) before the lag
            reaches the retention window. Do not lower retention to save disk
            while the connector is behind.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Connector stopped and will be down a while</th>
          <td>
            Time down against <code>expire_seconds</code>; managed services:
            the provider's own setting.
          </td>
          <td>
            Restart it, or raise retention <em>now</em>
            (<code>SET GLOBAL binlog_expire_logs_seconds = ...</code>, and
            persist it). Raising it cannot recover files already purged.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Updates arrive with only some columns, or tables stop producing events after a settings change</th>
          <td>Query 1: <code>binlog_format</code>, <code>binlog_row_image</code>.</td>
          <td>
            Set <code>ROW</code> and <code>FULL</code>. Changes already logged
            under another setting are incomplete in the sink: reconcile those
            tables.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Unexpected disconnects after adding another connector or replica</th>
          <td>
            Every <code>database.server.id</code> and every replica's
            <code>server_id</code> in the cluster.
          </td>
          <td>
            Give each client its own ID. (The uniqueness rule is documented; I
            did not find the exact symptom of a collision in the docs.)
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Connector fails on a DDL statement</th>
          <td>The log line naming the statement; the schema history topic exists.</td>
          <td>
            Prefer fixing the parse or the schema over
            <code>skip.unparseable.ddl</code>. Use
            <code>snapshot.mode=recovery</code> only if the history topic is
            lost and no schema changed since the last shutdown.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">After a primary change, errors or gaps</th>
          <td>
            GTID mode on both servers; <code>GTID_SUBSET</code> of the
            connector's set against the new server's
            <code>gtid_executed</code>.
          </td>
          <td>
            Wait for the new server to catch up, restart the connector, bump
            the sink's lineage number, and expect replayed changes.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="sqlserver" aria-labelledby="sqlserver-title">
    <h2 id="sqlserver-title">SQL Server: capture job, cleanup job and the validity interval</h2>
    <h3>Two jobs sit between a commit and your connector</h3>
    <p>
      Native CDC is enabled per database (<code>sys.sp_cdc_enable_db</code>)
      and then per table (<code>sys.sp_cdc_enable_table</code>), and needs SQL
      Server Agent running. Per Microsoft, the <strong>capture job</strong>
      reads the transaction log and writes rows into a change table; the
      <strong>cleanup job</strong> deletes old rows from it. Debezium does not
      read the log: each cycle it reads the change tables between its last
      stored maximum LSN and the current maximum, sorts by commit LSN and
      change LSN, and passes them as the offset to Kafka Connect. Debezium
      says SQL Server CDC &ldquo;is not designed to store a complete history of
      database changes&rdquo;, which is why there is a snapshot.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>CDC job defaults (Microsoft Learn) and what they mean for latency and retention</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Setting</th>
          <th scope="col">Default</th>
          <th scope="col">Effect</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Capture <code>pollinginterval</code></th>
          <td>5 seconds</td>
          <td>
            Wait between log scans. A higher value lowers load and raises
            latency; changes are not in the change table until a scan has
            processed them.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Capture <code>maxtrans</code>, <code>maxscans</code></th>
          <td>500 and 10 (see note)</td>
          <td>
            Transactions per scan cycle and cycles per drain. Throughput is
            bounded by (maxtrans &times; maxscans) &divide; polling interval.
            The <code>sp_cdc_add_job</code> reference and Debezium say 500; the
            &ldquo;What is change data capture&rdquo; overview page says
            1000. Read your own values with <code>sys.sp_cdc_help_jobs</code>.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Cleanup <code>retention</code></th>
          <td>4320 minutes (3 days)</td>
          <td>
            How long change rows are kept. One cleanup job serves all capture
            instances in the database, with one retention value.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Cleanup schedule, <code>threshold</code></th>
          <td>Daily at 2 A.M.; 5000</td>
          <td>
            Because it runs once a day, rows can outlive the retention value
            for a while. Do not plan on that margin. The threshold caps rows
            deleted per statement.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Debezium <code>poll.interval.ms</code></th>
          <td>500 ms</td>
          <td>
            How often the connector checks the change tables. Debezium caps
            the effective wait at 5000 ms. This adds to, and does not replace,
            the capture job's polling interval.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Changes to a job (<code>sys.sp_cdc_change_job</code>) do not take effect
      until the job is stopped and started again. Debezium also warns that the
      capture job's polling costs CPU on the database host, so lower latency is
      a trade, not a free setting.
    </p>
    <h3>Retention: the validity interval</h3>
    <p>
      Each capture instance has a validity interval: from its low endpoint
      (<code>sys.fn_cdc_get_min_lsn</code>, also the <code>start_lsn</code> in
      <code>sys.sp_cdc_help_change_data_capture</code>) to the high endpoint
      (<code>sys.fn_cdc_get_max_lsn</code>, the last LSN the capture process
      deposited). Microsoft: a request whose low end is left of the interval
      &ldquo;could&rdquo; miss data because of aggressive cleanup, and query
      functions fail for a range outside it. The cleanup job moves the low
      endpoint forward. It computes the new low point by subtracting the
      retention from the <em>latest captured commit time</em> (in
      <code>cdc.lsn_time_mapping</code>), not from the wall clock, so a stopped
      or slow <em>capture</em> job does not cause expiry. Your connector being
      slow does: the cleanup job knows nothing about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Debezium's own check, in its source on <code>main</code> (I did not read
      the 3.7 tag): at start it compares the oldest <code>start_lsn</code>
      across the capture instances with the stored commit LSN, and the stored
      position is valid only if the oldest is not newer than it. Debezium's
      <code>when_needed</code> snapshot mode is documented to snapshot when
      &ldquo;a previously recorded offset specifies a log position that is not
      available on the server&rdquo;. I did not find a sentence in the
      reference that says what the default <code>initial</code> mode logs in
      that situation, so check your version, and treat the position as lost
      either way.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- Raise retention BEFORE a planned outage, not after. 10080 minutes = 7 days: an example, not a recommendation.
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_change_job @job_type = N'cleanup', @retention = 10080;
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_stop_job   @job_type = N'cleanup';
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_start_job  @job_type = N'cleanup';</code></pre>
    <p>
      The cost of longer retention is change-table size; the cost of shorter
      retention is a lost position. Size it as policy: longer than the longest
      connector outage plus catch-up you intend to survive.
    </p>
    <h3>The log that will not truncate</h3>
    <p>
      The capture process tells the server's truncation logic how far it has
      read. Microsoft: with CDC enabled, even in the simple recovery model, the
      log truncation point &ldquo;will not advance until all the changes that
      are marked for capture have been gathered&rdquo;, and if the capture
      process is not running, <code>CHECKPOINT</code> will not truncate. So a
      stopped, failing or stalled capture job is SQL Server's version of the
      Postgres slot problem: the log grows. The error 9002 guide lists
      <code>REPLICATION</code> in <code>log_reuse_wait_desc</code> as the state
      to investigate for replication <em>or CDC</em> (and, in full recovery,
      log backups also gate truncation).
    </p>
    <h3 id="sqlserver-schema">Schema changes: two capture instances</h3>
    <p>
      A capture instance has a fixed column shape. Microsoft: columns added
      after enabling are ignored, a dropped tracked column yields nulls in later
      rows, a data type change is propagated, and DDL is recorded in
      <code>cdc.ddl_history</code>. A table can have at most <strong>two</strong>
      capture instances at once, so a schema change is: create the second
      instance with a unique <code>@capture_instance</code> name, let the
      connector move to it, then disable the old one. Debezium describes an
      offline procedure (stop the writers, wait for the connector to stream
      everything, stop it, apply the DDL, create the new instance, restart) and
      an online one with no downtime but a window in which events still carry
      the old shape. It also says that after a NULL/NOT NULL change the
      connector does not reflect optionality until a new instance exists, and
      that a table renamed with <code>sp_rename</code> keeps emitting under the
      old name until the connector restarts. Debezium also says SQL Server does
      not allow a column rename or type change on a CDC-enabled table, while
      Microsoft's overview says a type change is propagated to the change
      table: check your version.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- Online schema update (Debezium's documented pattern; SQL Server allows at most two instances per table)
ALTER TABLE dbo.customers ADD phone_number varchar(32);
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_enable_table @source_schema = N'dbo', @source_name = N'customers',
     @role_name = NULL, @supports_net_changes = 0, @capture_instance = N'dbo_customers_v2';
-- Wait for the connector's notification that it finished the old instance, then:
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_disable_table @source_schema = N'dbo', @source_name = N'customers',
     @capture_instance = N'dbo_customers';</code></pre>
    <p>
      Two instances capture the same change twice, with the same LSNs, so the
      position the sink compares does not change at the switch.
    </p>
    <h3 id="sqlserver-ag">Always On availability groups</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        CDC is configured on the current or intended primary. Debezium can read
        an Always On <em>read-only replica</em> (set
        <code>driver.applicationIntent=ReadOnly</code>; the isolation mode
        becomes <code>snapshot</code>), but CDC itself is not enabled on the
        replica.
      </li>
      <li>
        By default the log reader (which CDC uses) does not process log records
        that have not been hardened at all secondaries. A secondary that is
        down or behind can therefore hold capture back; Microsoft documents
        trace flag 1448 for asynchronous secondaries, which is a decision for
        your DBA, not a tip.
      </li>
      <li>
        After a failover, with CDC but no replication, the capture job does not
        follow the database: run <code>sys.sp_cdc_add_job</code> for
        <code>capture</code> and for <code>cleanup</code> on the new primary,
        and disable the CDC jobs on the old one (re-enable them if it becomes
        primary again).
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>sys.dm_cdc_log_scan_sessions</code> returns no rows on a
        secondary and is reset on restart or failover, so latency checks run on
        the primary. Debezium adds that on a read-only replica its transaction
        END marker comes only after the next transaction's first event.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="sqlserver-runbook" aria-labelledby="sqlserver-runbook-title">
    <h2 id="sqlserver-runbook-title">SQL Server triage and runbook</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Untested.</strong> There is no SQL Server in this repository's
      test setup. These statements were only parsed (node-sql-parser, T-SQL
      dialect). Read-only checks are safe to try; the
      <code>sp_cdc_*</code> calls change state, so use a test database first.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- 1. Is the Agent running, are both jobs there, what are their settings?
SELECT CASE WHEN dss.[status] = 4 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS agent_running   -- needs VIEW SERVER STATE
FROM sys.dm_server_services dss WHERE dss.[servicename] LIKE N'SQL Server Agent (%';
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_help_jobs;

-- 2. Capture instances and their low endpoints (start_lsn); at most two rows per table.
EXEC sys.sp_cdc_help_change_data_capture;

-- 3. Is capture keeping up? session_id 0 is the aggregate row.
SELECT TOP (5) session_id, start_time, end_time, tran_count,
       last_commit_cdc_time, latency, empty_scan_count, error_count
FROM sys.dm_cdc_log_scan_sessions ORDER BY session_id DESC;
SELECT TOP (20) entry_time, error_number, error_message
FROM sys.dm_cdc_errors ORDER BY entry_time DESC;

-- 4. Is the connector's position still inside the validity interval?
DECLARE @stored nvarchar(32) = N'00000027:00000758:0005';   -- commit_lsn from the connector's last event or offset
DECLARE @stored_lsn binary(10) = CONVERT(binary(10), '0x' + REPLACE(@stored, ':', ''), 1);
SELECT sys.fn_cdc_get_min_lsn(N'dbo_customers') AS low_endpoint,
       sys.fn_cdc_get_max_lsn()                 AS high_endpoint,
       @stored_lsn                              AS stored,
       CASE WHEN @stored_lsn >= sys.fn_cdc_get_min_lsn(N'dbo_customers')
            THEN 'inside' ELSE 'PURGED' END     AS verdict;
SELECT sys.fn_cdc_map_lsn_to_time(sys.fn_cdc_get_min_lsn(N'dbo_customers')) AS oldest_change_committed_at;

-- 5. Is the log being held back?
SELECT name, recovery_model_desc, log_reuse_wait_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE name = DB_NAME();
SELECT total_log_size_in_bytes, used_log_space_in_percent FROM sys.dm_db_log_space_usage;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Query 4 is my construction from the documented functions; the hex
      conversion uses style 1, which keeps the <code>0x</code> prefix. The
      CDC functions return <code>0x00000000000000000000</code> when the
      capture instance does not exist or the caller is not authorised, so a
      zero low endpoint means &ldquo;check the name and permissions&rdquo;, not
      &ldquo;nothing was purged&rdquo;.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>SQL Server: symptom, check, fix</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Symptom</th>
          <th scope="col">Check</th>
          <th scope="col">Fix</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Connector logs &ldquo;No maximum LSN recorded in the database; SQL Server Agent is not running&rdquo;</th>
          <td>Query 1. (Debezium: a missing maximum LSN is also normal on a new or very quiet database.)</td>
          <td>
            Start SQL Server Agent. While it is down no capture job runs and
            the log cannot truncate.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Events arrive late, change tables lag the source</th>
          <td>
            Query 3: <code>latency</code>, <code>empty_scan_count</code>,
            <code>error_count</code>; the job values from query 1.
          </td>
          <td>
            Tune <code>pollinginterval</code>, <code>maxtrans</code>,
            <code>maxscans</code> with <code>sp_cdc_change_job</code>, then
            stop and start the capture job. Lower latency costs CPU.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Connector was down longer than retention; position may be purged</th>
          <td>Query 4: verdict, and the oldest change time.</td>
          <td>
            If <code>PURGED</code>, the changes between are gone. Re-snapshot
            into the idempotent sink (<code>snapshot.mode=when_needed</code>,
            or clear the offset), then reconcile deletes. Raise retention with
            <code>sp_cdc_change_job</code> before the next outage.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Transaction log keeps growing, disk fills</th>
          <td>
            Query 5: <code>log_reuse_wait_desc</code>
            (<code>REPLICATION</code>); queries 1 and 3 for a stopped job or
            errors.
          </td>
          <td>
            Start or repair the capture job; in full recovery, keep log backups
            running. Do not disable CDC to free the log without planning the
            re-snapshot that follows.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">New column missing, or wrong nullability, in events</th>
          <td>Query 2: only the old instance exists.</td>
          <td>
            Create the second capture instance, let the connector move, drop the
            old one. If two already exist, drop the old one first.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Rows disappear from the table with no delete events (partition switch)</th>
          <td>
            Whether <code>ALTER TABLE ... SWITCH PARTITION</code> or similar
            bulk operations run on CDC tables.
          </td>
          <td>
            Microsoft: rows moved by a partition switch are not captured, so
            the change data is inconsistent with the table. Reconcile the sink
            after such operations (diff against the source).
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">After an availability-group failover, change tables stop filling</th>
          <td>Query 1 on the new primary: no capture or cleanup job.</td>
          <td>
            <code>EXEC sys.sp_cdc_add_job @job_type = N'capture'</code> and
            <code>N'cleanup'</code> on the new primary; disable the old
            primary's CDC jobs. Expect replayed events.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="guard" aria-labelledby="guard-title">
    <h2 id="guard-title">After a restart: duplicates and the position guard</h2>
    <p>
      Both Debezium references say the same thing: offsets are committed
      periodically, not when a change occurs, so after an outage or a crash the
      connector resumes from the last committed offset and re-emits what came
      after it. What becomes a duplicate:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The tail since the last committed offset.</strong> Same
        change, same position: <code>(commit_lsn, change_lsn,
        event_serial_no)</code> on SQL Server, <code>(file, pos, row)</code>
        on MySQL. Debezium's MySQL reference points at the binlog file and
        position (and GTIDs, if used) as the way to recognise a duplicate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A re-snapshot.</strong> Every captured row is emitted again as
        a read event. For MySQL, all of them carry the binlog position read at
        the start of the snapshot; for SQL Server, the maximum LSN read then.
        Rows unchanged in the meantime are a value-equal rewrite; rows deleted
        in the meantime are not removed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Snapshot signals.</strong> Debezium notes that after a blocking
        snapshot the connector might emit some events that duplicate rows the
        snapshot captured.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A move to another MySQL server.</strong> The same change can
        come again with the new server's coordinates, in a new lineage.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Heartbeats (<code>heartbeat.interval.ms</code>) can reduce the number of
      events re-sent after a restart, per the MySQL reference. They do not make
      delivery exactly-once.
    </p>
    <h3>Building the position for the guard</h3>
    <p>
      Key the target on the primary key, store the position next to each row,
      and apply a change only when its position is newer. Build one
      fixed-width, zero-padded text key per engine so a plain comparison
      is a typed comparison, and put a lineage number in front of it. The
      lineage number (<code>source_epoch</code>) is a counter <em>you</em>
      increment when the log lineage changes (a server switch, a binlog reset,
      a restore): positions are not comparable across lineages.
    </p>
    <pre><code>-- Target-side tables, written for PostgreSQL. The pattern is what matters; adapt the syntax to your warehouse.
CREATE TABLE customers_sink (
  id           bigint  PRIMARY KEY,
  email        text,
  deleted      boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false,   -- deletes stay as markers
  source_epoch int     NOT NULL,
  src_pos      text    NOT NULL
);

-- SQL Server event fields -> one comparable text key (8+8+4 hex digits per LSN, colons removed)
--   replace(coalesce(commit_lsn, '00000000:00000000:0000'), ':', '')
--   || replace(coalesce(change_lsn, '00000000:00000000:0000'), ':', '')
--   || lpad(coalesce(event_serial_no, 0)::text, 10, '0')

-- MySQL event fields -> one comparable text key (file NUMBER, not the name; then pos; then row)
--   lpad(substring(binlog_file from '[0-9]+$'), 10, '0')
--   || lpad(binlog_pos::text, 12, '0')
--   || lpad(coalesce(row_idx, 0)::text, 10, '0')

-- Apply one event ($1..$4 = id, email, op, epoch; $5 = the key built above).
INSERT INTO customers_sink AS t (id, email, deleted, source_epoch, src_pos)
VALUES ($1, $2, ($3 = 'd'), $4, $5)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE
SET email = EXCLUDED.email, deleted = EXCLUDED.deleted,
    source_epoch = EXCLUDED.source_epoch, src_pos = EXCLUDED.src_pos
WHERE (t.source_epoch, t.src_pos) &lt; (EXCLUDED.source_epoch, EXCLUDED.src_pos);</code></pre>
    <p>
      The guard is strict (<code>&lt;</code>), so an event with the same position
      does nothing: a replay is a no-op, and a late older event cannot overwrite
      a newer row. That is correct only because the tuple is unique per change;
      this is why <code>change_lsn</code> and <code>event_serial_no</code>, and
      the MySQL <code>row</code> index, are in the key and
      <code>commit_lsn</code> or <code>pos</code> alone are not.
      Padding matters because the file name and number are strings that
      compare wrongly otherwise: <code>'9' &lt; '10'</code> is false as text,
      and a MySQL file number is not guaranteed to keep its width forever.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="Tested on PostgreSQL, not on MySQL or SQL Server">
      <p>
        <strong>What was run.</strong> The guard and the key expressions ran on
        PGlite (PostgreSQL 18.3): an insert, an update in the same SQL Server
        transaction, replays of both, an older update arriving late, a delete,
        and a replay after the delete all ended on the delete marker; MySQL
        rows from one binlog event (same file and position, rows 0 and 1) and
        a file rollover from <code>.000009</code> to <code>.000010</code>
        ended on the newest state. The position <em>values</em> were
        hand-written to match the formats above; no real MySQL or SQL Server
        produced them.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <p>
      Rows to remember: a snapshot row is just an event with an older (or the
      snapshot) position, so the guard makes the snapshot and the stream
      converge; and deletes need their own position, so a replayed insert
      cannot resurrect a row. The worked patterns are on
      <a href="{{ '/partitioning/#recon' | url }}">Partitioning: version guards and watermarks</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/materialization/' | url }}">Materialization 101</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/snapshotting/#idempotent' | url }}">Snapshotting: idempotent apply</a>
      and the <a href="{{ '/merge-cookbook/' | url }}">Merge Cookbook</a>.
      Glossary:
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#idempotent-write' | url }}">idempotent write</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#effectively-once' | url }}">effectively-once</a>,
      <a href="{{ '/glossary/#backfill' | url }}">backfill</a>.
    </p>
    <aside class="callout" aria-label="No end-to-end exactly-once">
      <p>
        <strong>There is no exactly-once across the whole path.</strong> The
        connector delivers each change at least once. Whatever a component
        promises applies to its own hop; the pipeline as a whole is
        at-least-once delivery plus an idempotent sink, which reaches the same
        final state. See
        <a href="{{ '/exactly-once/' | url }}">Exactly-Once Semantics</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section id="checked" aria-labelledby="checked-title">
    <h2 id="checked-title">What was checked, and what was not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Read in the primary docs on 2026-10-09:</strong> the Debezium
        MySQL and SQL Server connector references (stable, labelled 3.7); MySQL
        8.4 pages for the binary log options, <code>PURGE BINARY LOGS</code>,
        <code>RESET BINARY LOGS AND GTIDS</code>, <code>SHOW BINARY LOGS</code>,
        <code>SHOW BINARY LOG STATUS</code>, GTID format, auto-positioning,
        GTID functions, the server error reference and the 8.4 removals list;
        the MySQL 8.0 page for <code>expire_logs_days</code>; Microsoft Learn
        pages for CDC concepts, <code>sp_cdc_enable_table</code>,
        <code>sp_cdc_add_job</code>, <code>sp_cdc_change_job</code>,
        <code>sp_cdc_help_jobs</code>, the CDC functions and views, the
        administer-and-monitor page, the transaction log guide, error 9002 and
        availability groups. Parts of the Debezium SQL Server connector source
        on <code>main</code> (the LSN string format, the field mapping, the
        position validation) were read as well.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Run:</strong> the guard SQL and the two key expressions, on
        PGlite 0.5.8 (PostgreSQL 18.3), with hand-written events. The MySQL
        and T-SQL statements were parse-checked with node-sql-parser 5.4.0:
        every statement parsed except <code>SHOW BINARY LOG STATUS</code>,
        <code>SHOW MASTER STATUS</code> and <code>SHOW BINLOG EVENTS</code>,
        which that parser does not support (taken from the manual instead).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not run:</strong> any MySQL or SQL Server instance, any
        Debezium connector, a real purge, a real cleanup, a real failover, an
        availability group. Everything described as behaviour of those systems
        comes from documentation, not from observation. In particular not
        verified: the text of the MySQL error 1236 for a purged file, what the
        SQL Server connector logs in <code>initial</code> mode when its
        position is below the low endpoint, whether the command id can disagree
        with the change LSN in direct mode, what Debezium reports when a new
        MySQL server lacks transactions the connector saw, and the symptom of a
        duplicate <code>server_id</code>. Settings that vary by Debezium
        release (<code>gtid.ignore.on.recovery</code>,
        <code>data.query.mode</code>, the snapshot-mode behaviour) are marked
        &ldquo;check your version&rdquo;.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not covered:</strong> managed services (RDS, Aurora, Azure SQL
        Database, whose CDC job mechanism differs) beyond what the docs say in
        passing; MariaDB (different GTIDs); Oracle (see
        <a href="{{ '/oracle-notes/' | url }}">Oracle notes</a>).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

